#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547103} :activity/id #uuid "d6022a2b-4bf1-5e53-8971-24eeeee3f3d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2010fc61-4364-5862-a96d-774075019bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} {:answer/id #uuid "705dbc1d-a754-523f-92e0-8940c3b04884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541650} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1b73b6-ad80-5c74-ab55-18bad8b8f274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541858} {:answer/id #uuid "d69ff15d-ef03-5444-8c1a-9ce4ef60002c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541881} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f93573-3c61-5ff8-b156-76483ae8624a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e60fe765-3ba7-5b2e-a3cc-67f6b659f5d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8d62c5b-3e1c-59af-b194-160b1241f14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143132" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to the cube of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the cube of its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the cube of its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{3}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08819b5d-c31d-5f8f-a25c-647533ce685e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "566b270e-601c-556c-909e-228f098bd523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500642} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb54fc0-7b29-5b8d-9591-693a024ce49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500902} {:answer/id #uuid "d478dfbd-05e0-5dc2-bc83-3caf65a81571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=kxy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500919} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c89197-063d-5efb-987f-b5a6be72e4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500962} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501013} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cf447a-a943-5b06-b1a6-df34fd52eceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143110" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2caacd20-fec4-513f-bebd-77f9f2aecd7e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa2b59f-32da-5f61-9e8c-c999179869a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373829} {:answer/id #uuid "77cab6e1-05d0-59ce-bfed-0248ceddcab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373830} {:answer/id #uuid "95aa500e-03cb-500e-ab89-a98ef4fc2170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373831} {:answer/id #uuid "34c6a305-ea32-55e9-8059-937e6d3212f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373832} {:answer/id #uuid "ffce36b0-40f6-5025-80cc-075308f5e7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373831} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa7450e-ccaf-5284-b104-8cbf050240f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2bf1eec-2fea-5deb-b983-28820449be19" :question/math-academy-id "q-143133" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its circumference. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **circumference**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *circumference.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{2\\pi r}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi r} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbacfd83-8629-5916-ac5d-e566d37cbba0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547175}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93daca08-5346-5c8d-9d08-4c8a920607fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373815} {:answer/id #uuid "2a63d060-2d42-5042-b169-688f85c60c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373816} {:answer/id #uuid "4a592f41-9583-5de8-a838-256ec9464b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373817} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab4dfc8-bdfb-5f3c-bdaf-6e28e990adca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373818} {:answer/id #uuid "c7baf46c-24c9-551d-8c0b-fa86bb82933a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373816} :answer-field/id #uuid "7631cd09-e0b1-5b28-8edc-c8fe29726e41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ebd4947c-3eb3-5ec1-a356-73f943c86767" :question/math-academy-id "q-143128" :question/problem "The volume of a cylinder increases at a rate proportional to the radius of its base. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that the height of the cylinder does not change and that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **radius of its base**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the radius of its base.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kr,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the height of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi rh\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =kr \\\\ 2\\pi rh\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =kr \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{kr}{2\\pi rh} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f0052809-97ca-51d0-969f-77faff2a4d64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc77990-b298-505f-b5b6-6eb164aa042c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=15({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373857} {:answer/id #uuid "45be93aa-c0b6-57c6-b243-903709eba10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=5\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373858} {:answer/id #uuid "a288bd4c-2041-5dc4-9891-077a8a8c988f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373859} {:answer/id #uuid "23b3abcf-2ffe-5f04-abca-39f486a5cac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373860} {:answer/id #uuid "e40cd54b-a715-5581-a060-b2d06897778b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373861} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f7efba-0461-52b6-9d0a-d0990d07f9c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b00489a-807a-5221-8dab-d57f2fcb225b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143138" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-3,4)$ equals $15.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=15$ when at the point $(-3,4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 5 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fa547646-60d3-574c-951d-eeb33327ad0d"}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546249}]}
